WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:05.749
Crossover movies like Freddy vs. Jason or
Alien vs. Predator take characters from two

00:00:05.749 --> 00:00:10.840
different franchises and pair them up, establishing
the characters as co-inhabitants of the same

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.040
cinematic universe.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:16.400
But some movie crossovers are so subtle you
might have missed them entirely.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.180
For example ...

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.280
Spy Kids &amp; Machete

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:24.180
That's right, the gadget-creating uncle from
Robert Rodriguez's Spy Kids series is the

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:28.490
same dude who later goes on a mission for
vengeance and blood in the violent Machete

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:32.180
and Machete Kills movies, also helmed by Rodriguez.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.340
In Spy Kids and its sequels, Danny Trejo's
"Uncle Machete" and his collection of super-cool

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:42.080
spy gadgets comes to the aid of the Cortez
children against a variety of enemies.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:48.420
In 2007, Rodriguez teamed up with Quentin
Tarantino for the horror double-feature Grindhouse,

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.579
which had fake trailers for nonexistent movies,
including one for Machete.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:56.680
"I cost the most, because I'm the best there
is."

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:01.360
After making the trailer, Rodriguez decided
to go ahead and give Uncle Machete his own

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:06.920
full-length film — a rare case of PG and
R-rated films sharing the same universe.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.660
Trading Places &amp; Coming to America

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.550
In 1983's Trading Places, Dan Aykroyd and
Eddie Murphy are forced to switch lives through

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.451
the orchestrations of the nefarious broker
brothers Randolph and Mortimer Duke.The movie

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:24.300
ends with the Duke brothers' business ruined,
leaving them destitute.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:29.420
Five years later, another Eddie Murphy movie
would show us what became of the Dukes after

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:30.200
their downfall.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:36.110
In 1988's Coming to America, Murphy is a different
character: an incredibly wealthy prince who

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:40.700
travels to the United States in search of
a bride who will love him for who he is rather

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:41.750
than for his money.

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:45.970
To further that goal, the prince gives all
his remaining money to a pair of homeless

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:49.300
men—who turn out, somehow, to be the Duke
brothers.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:55.020
"Mortimer, we're back!"

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:57.540
yea!

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:00.080
Star Wars &amp; E.T.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:05.800
This sci-fi crossover was deliberately planned
by both Steven Spielberg and George Lucas.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.840
First, there's the scene from E.T. where the
kids encounter a kid dressed in a Yoda costume,

00:02:10.840 --> 00:02:12.590
reminding E.T. of where he came from ...

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:13.600
"Home!"

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.420
That's… obviously not the REAL ancient Jedi,
but E.T. clearly recognizes the guy.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.980
Spielberg's buddy Lucas returned the favor
in 1999, when he included a delegation of

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.740
E.T. aliens as members of the Galactic Senate.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.070
According to an Extended Universe novella,
the aliens are known as Asogians and their

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:37.520
leader in the Senate was named Grebleips–"Spielberg"
spelled backward.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.320
The Rules of Attraction &amp; American Psycho

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.810
If you're familiar with the source material
for these two very different movies, then

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:48.580
you probably already know about the connection
between The Rules of Attraction and American

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:51.750
Psycho, both based on Bret Easton Ellis novels.

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:57.300
If you're not, then here's a brief explanation:
James van der Beek's Sean Bateman in 2002's

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:01.750
The Rules of Attraction is actually the younger
brother of Christian Bale's Patrick Bateman

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.600
from 2000's American Psycho.

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:08.100
Patrick was supposed to make a brief appearance
in The Rules of Attraction, played by another

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.720
actor, but all of his scenes were cut before
the movie was released.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.080
The Lone Ranger &amp; The Green Hornet

00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:18.840
While it can't be acknowledged openly today
onscreen by either of the characters, the

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.660
Lone Ranger and The Green Hornet are actually
related.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:26.580
In the early radio serials, it was established
that Britt Reid, The Green Hornet, was the

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:29.000
descendant of John Reid, The Lone Ranger.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:33.720
Depending on the source, John is described
as either Britt's uncle or great uncle.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.720
Much later, the properties were sold to different
studios, so the characters can't intermingle

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:39.720
on the big screen.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.520
But while movie crossovers are out, the comic
book rights are a slightly different story.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.690
In 2016, Dynamite Comics obtained the rights
to both characters and published a crossover

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.720
comic book series that explores their familial
connection.

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.620
Tarantino's dual cinematic universes

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.990
For years, movie fans have speculated that
all of Quentin Tarantino's movies take place

00:04:00.990 --> 00:04:07.200
within the same universe, and Tarantino himself
confirmed in 2016 that the theory is mostly

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:07.700
correct.

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.420
There are actually two universes within his
films — one is the "real" world in which

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:16.690
many of the characters live, and the other
is the "movie" world containing films that

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.040
the people in the "real" world would go and
see.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.720
As Tarantino explains, "So when all the characters
of Reservoir Dogs or Pulp Fiction, when they

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.580
go to the movies, Kill Bill is what they go
to see.

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:28.340
From Dusk Till Dawn is what they see."

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:33.780
The logic isn't airtight, but here's just
one example: In Pulp Fiction, actress Mia

00:04:33.790 --> 00:04:38.940
Wallace, played by Uma Thurman, says she was
in a TV pilot about female secret agents where

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:42.970
she plays "the deadliest woman in the world
with a knife" — a reference to Thurman's

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:44.880
character, The Bride, in Kill Bill.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.220
This means the drug-addled Mia Wallace cleaned
up her act to play "The Bride" within the

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:54.100
Tarantino universe, making Kill Bill more
than just a spiritual sequel to Pulp Fiction.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:55.800
Thanks for watching!

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.650
